riscv64 zkVM: spill around calls on cold paths instead of callee-saved registers

On the zkVM every executed instruction is one step, so a callee-saved register costs its
save and restore on every invocation (plus the move out of the argument register for a
parameter), whereas a caller-saved register costs a store before and a reload after each
call that actually executes. LSRA always preferred callee-saved registers for a local live
across any call, and when it did spill such a local it stored it at the last reference
before the call (usually on the hot path) and reloaded it at the next use in the join
block (hot again), so plain spilling never paid off.

JitZkSpillKeepReg (default 0): a local whose caller-saved register is killed by a call
keeps the register up to that call. The allocation pass marks the spilling reference
(spilledByKill) only when the interval is active and this very kill causes the spill (an
evicted "previous interval" already carries spillAfter); the write-back pass keeps the
interval live until the RefTypeKill, where it retires every kept local in a killed register,
so the block's outgoing location still names the register and resolution places the reload
on the edges leaving the call's block; codegen (genCall) stores each kept local to its home
right before the call instruction and drops it from the register and GC sets, using the
call's real kill set (none for a perf-56 CSR precompile pseudo-call). A reloaded reference
that is kept needs no second store; single-def locals are no longer stored at their
definition when the only spill is such a kill. verifyFinalAllocation mirrors the new state.
Parameters stay in their argument register; EH-live and reg-optional references are not
kept.

JitZkColdCallSpill=<percent> (default 0): instead of preferring callee-saved registers at
every call, the summed (synthesized) weight of the call blocks a local is live across is
compared, once all RefPositions are built, with that percentage of the entry block weight
(150% of it for parameters); below the threshold the local keeps its caller-saved candidates
and is spilled around the calls with the mechanism above.

Measured on nine mainnet blocks on top of perf-62 (JitZkSynthesizeCounts=1): keep-reg
alone changes nothing (the callee-save preference still wins); keep-reg with the policy at
125% (the value bflat passes) gives -0.74% executed steps (-1.19% together with perf-62,
1,523,663,934 = -10.56% versus master); 50% -0.43%, 80% -0.62%, 100% -0.71%, 150% -0.46%,
200% -0.31%; the policy without perf-62 -0.72%; the policy with plain LSRA spilling loses
(+0.25..0.4%). Outputs identical.

